 ],
 "answer_text": "Found 5 pubmed records: On tree longevity. [4], Tree mycorrhizal type and tree diversity shape the forest soil microbiota. [5], Gradient Boosted Tree Approaches for Mapping European Organization for Research and Treatment of Cancer Quality of Life Questionnaire Core 30 Onto 5-Level Version of EQ-5D Index for Patients With Cancer. [6], Tree diversity effects on productivity depend on mycorrhizae and life strategies in a temperate forest experiment. [7] and Seasonal patterns of nonstructural carbohydrate storage and mobilization in two tree species with distinct life-history traits. [8]. \n\nTree longevity\n\nTrees do not appear to die from genetically programmed meristem senescence but are instead killed by external agents or disturbance events [1]. Long lifespans are enabled by life-history trait combinations within realized niches that confer resistance to or avoidance of extrinsic mortality [1]. \n\nMycorrhizal type and forest productivity\n\nPositive relationships between tree species richness and community productivity strengthen over time, but arbuscular and ectomycorrhizal tree species influence productivity differently [2]. Ectomycorrhizal communities show low initial productivity with increasing increment over time and strong biodiversity effects, whereas arbuscular communities display the opposite pattern [2]. \n\nNonstructural carbohydrate dynamics\n\nBranches and roots serve as the most dynamic NSC-supplying organs in the respective species [3]. \n\nPubmed records found\n\nPubmed title: On tree longevity [4]. Pubmed title: Tree mycorrhizal type and tree diversity shape the forest soil microbiota [5]. Pubmed title: Gradient Boosted Tree Approaches for Mapping European Organization for Research and Treatment of Cancer Quality of Life Questionnaire Core 30 Onto 5-Level Version of EQ-5D Index for Patients With Cancer [6]. Pubmed title: Tree diversity effects on productivity depend on mycorrhizae and life strategies in a temperate forest experiment [7]. Pubmed title: Seasonal patterns of nonstructural carbohydrate storage and mobilization in two tree species with distinct life-history traits [8]. "
